                <pb facs="1002" n="688" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg688"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111/721</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Ecuador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hartman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Quito</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1914-11-19T10:00:00-05:14">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">November 19, 1914, 10 a.m.</hi>
                    </date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received November 20, 3 p.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>Your November 14, 7 p.m. and November 16, 6 p.m.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1101-f1"/>
                    I presented subject to the Minister for Foreign Affairs yesterday. He informed
                    me he did not know whether the Germans had made use of the Galápagos Islands but
                    that, if true, it was not with the previous knowledge of Ecuador. He showed me
                    copies of orders to naval officers which he said had been sent, instructing them
                    to observe strictly the neutrality proclamation. He declares that the Government
                    will investigate the charges and if found to be [?], will make a protest to the
                    German Government. He said that owing to the weakness of Ecuador and the present
                    revolution, Ecuador could not do more than protest. The British and French
                    Ministers express the belief that the Ecuadorian Government has done and is
                    doing all in its power to enforce its proclamation of neutrality. Considering
                    the distance of the islands from the mainland and from each other, the absence
                    of telegraphic communication, and the lack of vessels, it is impossible to keep
                    informed about them.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hartman</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
